        "title": "HONESTY? EMPLOYABILITY?",
        "description": "<p>You know, I was thinking to myself, that someday soon, when I’m an employer of labour and/or when I’m at the level where I interview young minds for a job, <br>I’d wait, after they’re done presenting themselves as the best fit, and ask “…but it seems you are doing well at your current place, so, why do you want to leave?” Or “…but there’s news that your current place is a good place to work, so, why do you want to leave?</p> <p>I’m asking, not because I want to have my lick back, and judge them by it, like someone recently did me, but because I want to genuinely listen, and hear their unsaid words, like someone recently did me (been at the end of both barrels…)…’cos trust me, you might just see the glimmer disappear from your faces, a lowered tone, a crack in their voice.</p> <p> Not because they don’t know why they’re leaving, but how do they explain it to you without you branding them, judging them. Since it’s UNPROFESSIONAL and bad marketing of yourself to say “the work-environment became a little too toxic for me”, “I had a new boss who was more difficult to work with” “the work culture did not allow me to have an attempt at balancing life and work”…even career coaches and AI chatbots advise against such answers… </p> <p> I mean, those answers are often interpreted as though you're not as resilient or high-performing as you've presented yourself to be in the last 45minutes of the interview. And trust me, they know, you can’t play victim at life’s games, but some times, most times, they know - they know when you’re the victim at that particular game,  when you’re just one caught in a web. </p> <p>Especially if you’re still shuffling the same industry…Sometimes, they’ve been caught in the same web as you, but aren’t we all just on our high horses…“…do you mean we have lesser workload here?” “…do you think we don’t chase performance here?”Maybe it’s a valid question to evaluate you, judge professionalism, fact check your reasoning; but it can easily be a trap rather than an invitation to understand someone's story, especially when the answer once it does not have that ‘go getter, driven’ loudly inscribed on it.</p>   <p>*************second thought ************</p> <p>I WON’T!!!I WILL NOT DO IT!!!To what point?“…but it seems you are doing well at your current place, so, why do you want to leave?”Did they not know about that? Did they not just spend the last 45minutes tooting their own horn to you, convincing you they’re your best bet? Ofcourse, they know they’re doing well, and they know about their application to yours, they’re not absent at the interview. They’re there with you!!! Even if the application was sent in proxy, it wasn’t their clone you just finished addressing (well, except this AI thing is now crazy and we now scam our way out interviews too 🤷🏽‍♀️)“…but there’s news that your current place is a good place to work, so, why do you want to leave?”If you can know that much, then they do too.</p> And, maybe with a little bit more information than you do. What if the title was a sham? Or what if there’s something they need now, that your organization might just be able to give them, which they cannot just get as fast at their current place? And do you mean your place is the toxic place to work? Well, maybe it is, ‘cos that question might just be the ‘ticker’ they need…</p> <p>All that to say, they know they’re doing well there, they’d just rather not stay, but they’ve chosen to leave and come to yours. And, don’t you need staff afterall? Don’t you need high flyers too? And they know they’re leaving their ‘good place to work’, maybe they just want to leave their comfort zone for a bitPft!All that to say, I don’t know the conclusion to draw from this, just, draw your conclusion, do with the article what you wish. <p> As the triggering questions or not, it’s your call, your recruitment process, your organization!!!All I know is, mental health, has “health” with it for a reason. And health, is wealth, hence, should be valid enough.<br>Maybe the question shouldn’t be why they're leaving. What if it’s focused on if your organization can become what they're looking for? What would you want here that wasn’t there? Might just be less of a trigger, arriving at the same insight. Who knows, they might even tell you why they’re leaving.<br>But, what do I know? Till I sit, on an interview panel!!!Peace!!!!</p>",
